    <body>
      <sec id="article-623.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Mitral valve repair addresses mitral valve disease, including stenosis and regurgitation, that impairs cardiac function and reduces effective forward flow. The primary goal involves restoration of normal valvular function while preserving native tissue to improve survival and quality of life. Reconstructive strategies include annuloplasty, leaflet resection, chordal replacement with polytetrafluoroethylene, and transcatheter techniques tailored to specific pathology. Participants learn to evaluate mitral valve disease, interpret echocardiographic findings, and select appropriate therapeutic pathways in clinical practice. Interprofessional collaboration among clinicians, cardiology specialists, imaging experts, and surgeons enhances perioperative planning, decision-making, and patient safety. This approach improves outcomes and supports durable repair success in appropriately selected patients. Learners strengthen clinical decision-making skills and apply evidence-based principles to optimize long-term management of mitral valve disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the relevant anatomy, indications, and contraindications for mitral valve repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select&#x000a0;the equipment, personnel, preparation, and technique regarding mitral valve repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate&#x000a0;the potential complications and clinical significance of mitral valve repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate&#x000a0;interprofessional team strategies for improving care and communication to advance mitral valve repair and improve outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=623&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=623">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Although the standard of care for degenerative mitral valve disease is surgical repair, patient outcomes depend on several variables, including preoperative status, mitral regurgitation severity, procedural technique, and the surgeon's and center's experience. In appropriately selected patients who undergo timely mitral repair, operative risk is low, and long-term survival approaches that of age- and sex-matched populations. In high-risk individuals, choosing among surgical, percutaneous, and conservative strategies is complex and should be individualized based on comorbid conditions, anatomic suitability, and local expertise. Mitral valve repair generally offers important advantages over valve replacement, but patient-specific clinical and anatomic factors must guide final decision-making. Notably, a substantial proportion&#x02014;on the order of one-half&#x02014;of patients with severe mitral valve disease are not candidates for surgery, most commonly because of advanced age, frailty, or significant comorbidities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Up to 3% of adults in the United States have&#x000a0;mitral valve disease (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Insufficient Mitral Valve).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r4">[4]</xref> Patients with degenerative pathology who develop symptoms of mitral regurgitation have a poor prognosis, with annual mortality rates of up to 34%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r5">[5]</xref> Mitral stenosis, primarily caused by rheumatic heart disease, is commonly treated with percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (also called percutaneous mitral balloon valvotomy) or mitral valve replacement. Repair is usually not feasible in patients with rheumatic mitral disease.</p>
        <p>Mitral regurgitation is classified as primary or secondary, depending on whether the lesion is located at the valvular apparatus or due to left ventricular changes, respectively. While severe primary&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation still receives treatment with surgical intervention, percutaneous techniques for repair and replacement are also gaining traction. Mitral valve replacement may be considered in patients with&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation due to papillary muscle rupture, degenerative or ischemic mitral regurgitation, or those with a failed repair undergoing reoperation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Anatomy of the Mitral Valve</bold>
</p>
        <p>The mitral valve apparatus has a complex anatomy, including an annulus,&#x000a0;2 leaflets,&#x000a0;3 types of chordae tendinae, and&#x000a0;2 papillary muscles (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Mitral Valve Anatomy). The mitral annulus, shaped like a "D," is at the junction of the left atrium, left ventricle, and mitral leaflets.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The left circumflex artery runs laterally around the mitral annulus. At the same time, the coronary sinus lies posteriorly, and both play a role in supplying blood to the mitral leaflets (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Mitral Valve, Transverse View).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The mitral valve annulus is a saddle-shaped structure, consistent with that of the aortic valve. The annulus is most vulnerable to dilatation at its insertion on the posterior leaflet because it is the thinnest at this junction. There are&#x000a0;2 mitral valve leaflets: the anterior is tall and narrow, and the posterior is shorter and broader. These&#x000a0;2 leaflets meet at their respective commissures, known as the anterolateral and posteromedial commissures.</p>
        <p>Each leaflet is divided into&#x000a0;3 scallops, 1 to 3, from lateral to medial, and they are designated A1, A2, A3 (anterior) and P1, P2, and P3 (posterior).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;The anterior leaflet occupies two-thirds of the valvular area and one-third of the annular area, whereas the posterior leaflet comprises two-thirds of the annular circumference. The leaflets have a normal line of coaptation from 7 to 9 mm, allowing for a range of normal physiologic pressures and volumes. The posterior, or mural leaflet, is more vulnerable to prolapse because it is attached to the ventricular free wall, exposing it to the recurrent stress of ventricular contraction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Chordae tendinae, fibrous structures that connect the mitral leaflets to the papillary muscles, come in&#x000a0;3 types based on their insertion: primary (attached to the leaflet's free margin to prevent prolapse), secondary (inserted on the rough surface of the leaflet), and tertiary (attached to the basal portion). The&#x000a0;2 papillary muscles are anterolateral and posteromedial, with the posteromedial muscle receiving blood from the posterior descending artery and the anterolateral muscle receiving dual supply from the left anterior descending artery and the left circumflex artery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r9">[9]</xref> These valvular components work in concert to maintain proper blood flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle.</p>
        <p>A normal mitral valve area measures 4 to 6 cm&#x000b2;. In cases of mitral regurgitation, the left ventricle often becomes thin, dilated, and dysfunctional, with an increased risk of arrhythmias. Anomalies of the mitral valve apparatus are increasingly recognized as a phenotypic expression of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r7">[7]</xref> These anomalies include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anterior displacement of the mitral valve</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Elongated mitral leaflets
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The anterior mitral leaflet (AML) is often longer than 30 mm, with some cases exceeding 40 mm, compared to an average of 25 mm in controls. The posterior mitral leaflet (PML) is also elongated, typically measuring more than 17 mm. The elongation of the AML shifts the coaptation plane toward its body rather than near the free edge, causing the distal portion of the AML to bend into the left ventricular cavity during systole. This results in a sharp angulation of the distal AML toward the septum in midsystole. A residual distal AML and abnormal leaflet coaptation are necessary to develop systolic anterior motion and left ventricle outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Results from a cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study found that AML length in those with HCM averaged 26 &#x000b1; 5 mm&#x000a0;(range 17&#x02013;41 mm), significantly longer than in controls, which averaged 19 &#x000b1; 5 mm&#x000a0;(range 8&#x02013;29 mm). The PML length in patients with HCM was 14 &#x000b1; 4 mm (range, 6&#x02013;28 mm), which also significantly exceeded that of controls at 10 &#x000b1; 3 mm (range, 2&#x02013;17 mm). A ratio of AML length to transverse LVOT diameter greater than 2.0 was more common in patients with LVOT gradients exceeding 30 mm Hg at rest.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Excessive AML area</bold>&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r11">[11]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Papillary muscle anomalies
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Over 50% of patients with HCM exhibit anomalies in the papillary muscles, which may include:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypertrophy of papillary muscle heads with or without accompanying septal or posterior wall hypertrophy, potentially leading to mid-cavity obstruction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Increased number of papillary muscles; greater than 50% of those with HCM have 3 to 4 papillary muscle heads.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r12">[12]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior and apical displacement of the papillary muscles, which shifts the mitral leaflets toward the LVOT, contributes to chordal and leaflet laxity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r12">[12]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Direct insertion of papillary muscle&#x000a0;onto the ventricular aspect of the AML is observed in approximately 13% of patients with HCM.</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Degenerative, myxomatous, and restrictive valves
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A study of 851 patients with HCM who underwent surgery at the Cleveland Clinic revealed that 115 required a concomitant mitral valve procedure. The most common abnormalities were degenerative changes (31%), myxomatous degeneration (20%), papillary muscle anomalies (20%), chordal restriction (19%), leaflet restriction (70%), and abnormally long leaflets (56%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r13">[13]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Physiology of&#x000a0;Mitral Stenosis and Mitral Regurgitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The pathophysiology of mitral stenosis and mitral regurgitation centers on the disruption of normal mitral valve function, leading to significant hemodynamic changes. In mitral stenosis, restricted valve opening impairs blood flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle, while in mitral regurgitation, valve incompetence causes retrograde blood flow during systole, contributing to progressive cardiac dysfunction.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mitral stenosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Rheumatic heart disease is a common cause of mitral stenosis, often linked to an exaggerated immune response due to cross-reactivity between streptococcal antigens and valvular tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;This condition leads to characteristic changes such as a "fish-mouth" appearance of the mitral valve orifice, commissural fusion, leaflet thickening, and fusion of chordae tendinae, culminating in a "hockey-stick" appearance of the anterior leaflet on echocardiography&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold> Treatment Flowchart for Rheumatic Mitral Valve Stenosis).</p>
        <p>Other causes of mitral stenosis include radiation-induced valvulitis, systemic inflammatory disorders, obstructive lesions, infectious vegetations, congenital valvular abnormalities, and mitral annular calcification, prevalent in older patients or those with advanced renal disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Progressive mitral stenosis, especially with a valve area of less than 2 cm&#x000b2;, leads to a diastolic pressure gradient between the left atrium and left ventricle, resulting in increased left atrium pressure and potentially reduced forward flow. Tachycardia exacerbates this condition by decreasing diastolic filling time and increasing the transmitral gradient. Elevated left atrium pressures can cause atrial enlargement, increasing the risk of thromboembolism and arrhythmias, particularly atrial fibrillation. Additionally, elevated pulmonary pressures can lead to pulmonary edema, hypertension, right ventricular failure, and tricuspid regurgitation, while decreased forward flow results in poor left ventricular filling and reduced cardiac output.</p>
        <p>To quantify mitral stenosis, clinicians use echocardiography to assess the mean diastolic transmitral pressure gradient, valvular area, and sizes and pressures of the LA and right-sided chambers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;The mean diastolic pressure gradient is measured with continuous-wave Doppler from an apical&#x000a0;4-chamber view, with velocity measurements converted to pressure using the Bernoulli equation. Severe mitral stenosis is indicated by a mean gradient of 10 mm Hg or more, moderate mitral stenosis by 5 to 10 mm Hg, and mild mitral stenosis by a gradient below 5 mm Hg. The 2020&#x000a0;American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association guidelines classify mitral stenosis as very severe if the mitral valve area is 1 cm&#x000b2; or less and severe if it is 1.5 cm&#x000b2; or less.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mitral regurgitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mitral regurgitation can be categorized as primary or secondary, depending on whether the abnormality originates from the valvular apparatus or the left ventricle. In&#x000a0;low-resource countries, mitral valve prolapse is the most common cause of MR requiring surgical repair, whereas degenerative disease is more prevalent in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Mitral valve prolapse is characterized by systolic displacement of the leaflet(s) more than 2 mm above the mitral annulus plane on a long-axis view.</p>
        <p>Barlow disease, marked by mucopolysaccharide accumulation and fibroelastic deficiency, compromises the mechanical integrity of the mitral valve. Primary mitral regurgitation may also result from infective endocarditis, mitral annular calcification, rheumatic heart disease, connective tissue disorders, congenital anomalies, and drug use. Secondary mitral regurgitation, or functional mitral regurgitation, is associated with left ventricular remodeling, mitral annular dilation, and impaired left ventricular contractility.</p>
        <p>Secondary&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation can be further divided into ischemic and nonischemic categories. Ischemic&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation due to left ventricular dysfunction from coronary artery disease (CAD) often signifies a poor prognosis.&#x000a0;Nonischemic mitral regurgitation arises from various cardiomyopathies or atrial fibrillation with subsequent annular dilation.</p>
        <p>Ischemic mitral regurgitation is a serious complication of CAD and is associated with substantially increased mortality and morbidity. Although optimal guideline-directed medical therapy and coronary revascularization (PCI or CABG) are central to the management of moderate-to-severe ischemic mitral regurgitation, the optimal surgical strategy for the valve itself remains controversial. Mitral valve repair provides clear clinical benefits in primary (degenerative) mitral regurgitation, but these advantages are less consistent in functional ischemic&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation because the underlying mechanism is fundamentally different.</p>
        <p>In ischemic mitral regurgitation, the valve leaflets are usually structurally normal; instead, left ventricular remodeling with papillary muscle displacement, leaflet tethering, and impaired coaptation, demonstrated in echocardiographic and mechanistic studies, drives regurgitation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Neither repair nor replacement directly reverses the adverse left ventricular geometry that produces ischemic mitral regurgitation, and randomized and cohort data have shown similar survival but higher rates of recurrent&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation after repair than after replacement, suggesting that replacement may be more durable in some populations. However, newer subvalvular techniques aimed at correcting papillary muscle displacement and leaflet tethering have improved the durability of repair in selected patients, and contemporary guidelines and reviews still emphasize individualized decision-making based on ventricular geometry, comorbidities, and surgical expertise. As a result, the debate over the optimal surgical strategy for ischemic&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation remains unresolved, with increasing consideration of transcatheter options in high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Depending on whether the mitral regurgitation is acute or chronic and on the magnitude of the regurgitant volume, it leads to left atrium and left ventricular volume overload. Acute mitral regurgitation leads to a sudden increase in preload and left ventricular filling pressures that can cause pulmonary edema. Cardiac output is reduced since blood flow is directed to the left ventricle, which can precipitate cardiogenic shock; this is a joint presentation in patients with infective endocarditis, chordae tendinae rupture, or papillary muscle rupture following myocardial infarction.</p>
        <p>Chronic mitral regurgitation is divided into&#x000a0;3 stages: a compensated stage, in which most patients are asymptomatic; a transitional stage with left ventricular remodeling; and a decompensated stage marked by the insidious development of symptoms. Chronic mitral regurgitation also leads to left atrial enlargement, which increases the risk of atrial arrhythmias and thromboembolic events. Carpentier&#x000a0;developed a functional classification of&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation based on leaflet motion (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Carpentier Functional Classification of Mitral Regurgitation). Results from the Olmsted County community study showed that&#x000a0;54% of patients with moderate-to-severe&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation also had atrial fibrillation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Echocardiography is the primary imaging modality for grading the severity of mitral regurgitation and guiding management.&#x000a0;Color Doppler evaluation of mitral regurgitation includes assessment of the regurgitant jet area and its ratio to the left atrial area; a jet occupying more than 40% of the left ventricle is generally consistent with severe mitral regurgitation, although comprehensive assessment must also incorporate other semi-quantitative and quantitative parameters. In severe mitral regurgitation, transmitral inflow typically shows a dominant E-wave with peak velocity around or above 1.2 m/s, and pulmonary venous Doppler often demonstrates systolic flow blunting or reversal, which is highly specific for severe regurgitation.</p>
        <p>Progressive worsening of mitral regurgitation is accompanied by a transition from systolic dominance to systolic blunting and then frank systolic flow reversal in the pulmonary veins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Prompt surgical intervention is crucial in patients with severe mitral regurgitation and preserved left ventricular ejection fraction in order to prevent irreversible adverse remodeling of the left ventricle and deterioration of prognosis. Biomarkers and advanced functional indices, including B-type natriuretic peptide, left ventricular global longitudinal strain, exercise capacity, and right ventricular systolic pressure and pulmonary artery pressure, provide important incremental prognostic information and can help refine the timing of intervention in asymptomatic or borderline patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>The American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association (ACC/AHA)&#x000a0;guidelines provide specific criteria for managing MS&#x000a0;and mitral regurgitation, particularly regarding mitral valve repair (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Indications for Intervention in Severe Primary Mitral Regurgitation).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;In this course, guideline statements follow the European Society of Cardiology/American College of Cardiology convention, where Class I&#x02013;III labels indicate the strength of recommendation (from clearly beneficial to not recommended).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;The relevant guidelines for each condition are summarized here.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mitral Stenosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mitral valve repair for mitral stenosis is less common than for mitral regurgitation, and the primary intervention for mitral stenosis is typically percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (PMBC). However, valve repair or surgical commissurotomy is considered in specific situations where PMBC is not feasible. The ACC/AHA guidelines include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Symptomatic patients with severe mitral stenosis (class I)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients with severe symptomatic mitral stenosis (valve area &#x02264;1.5 cm&#x000b2;) who are not candidates for PMBC due to unfavorable valve anatomy, or those with contraindications such as a left atrium thrombus, should be considered for mitral valve repair or surgical commissurotomy.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe mitral stenosis with new-onset atrial fibrillation (class IIa)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>For patients with severe mitral stenosis who develop new-onset atrial fibrillation or have recurrent systemic embolization, mitral valve repair or replacement should be considered if PMBC is not an option.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pulmonary hypertension in severe mitral stenosis (class IIa)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In patients with asymptomatic severe mitral stenosis who develop pulmonary hypertension, defined as a pulmonary artery systolic pressure &#x0003e;50 mm Hg, surgical intervention, including mitral valve repair, is recommended when PMBC is not appropriate.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Symptomatic moderate mitral stenosis (class IIb)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Surgical mitral valve repair may be considered in patients with moderate mitral stenosis (mitral valve area &#x0003e;1.5 cm&#x000b2; but &#x0003c;2 cm&#x000b2;) who are symptomatic and have other cardiac indications for surgery, such as concomitant coronary artery bypass grafting.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Mitral Regurgitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mitral valve repair is often preferred over replacement&#x000a0;for mitral regurgitation, especially in primary mitral regurgitation. The ACC/AHA guidelines emphasize early intervention in certain patient populations to prevent long-term complications. The key recommendations include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Symptomatic severe primary mitral regurgitation (class I)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Surgical mitral valve repair is recommended for symptomatic individuals with severe primary&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation and preserved left ventricular function (ejection fraction [EF] &#x0003e;30%).</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Asymptomatic severe primary mitral regurgitation with left ventricular dysfunction (class I)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In asymptomatic individuals with severe primary mitral regurgitation and evidence of left ventricular dysfunction (LVEF &#x02264;60% or&#x000a0;left ventricular end-systolic diameter &#x02265;40 mm), early surgical intervention with mitral valve repair is strongly recommended to prevent further left ventricular remodeling and heart failure.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe primary mitral stenosis with atrial fibrillation or pulmonary hypertension (class IIa)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Asymptomatic individuals with severe primary mitral regurgitation and preserved left ventricular function who develop new-onset atrial fibrillation or resting pulmonary hypertension, as defined above, should be considered for mitral valve repair.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Asymptomatic severe primary mitral regurgitation with normal left ventricular function and high likelihood of durable repair (class IIa)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Early mitral valve repair may be considered in patients with asymptomatic, severe primary mitral regurgitation and normal left ventricular function, with a high likelihood of successful, durable repair. This is especially relevant if the surgical team has a high success rate with repair procedures.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe secondary mitral regurgitation (class IIb)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>For patients with severe secondary mitral regurgitation and heart failure, mitral valve repair may be considered if the patient is undergoing another cardiac surgery, such as CABG or aortic valve surgery. However, depending on the underlying cause and left ventricular function, mitral valve replacement may sometimes be more appropriate, especially in patients with ischemic mitral regurgitation.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Moderate secondary mitral regurgitation in patients undergoing other cardiac surgery (class IIa)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In patients with moderate secondary mitral regurgitation undergoing surgery for another cardiac condition, such as CABG or aortic valve replacement, mitral valve replacement should be considered to address mitral regurgitation and improve long-term outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>A cardiac surgeon experienced in mitral valve surgery should evaluate all patients with severe mitral regurgitation to determine the optimal intervention and timing. Standard preoperative assessment includes a detailed review of coronary anatomy, comorbid conditions, and prior cardiac or thoracic operations, and is typically complemented by validated risk models such as the Society of Thoracic Surgeons risk calculator to estimate operative mortality and major morbidity. Several conditions are considered relative contraindications to mitral valve repair because they increase technical difficulty and perioperative risk, or may limit the benefit of surgery. These include significant aortic calcification, right ventricular dysfunction, and severe mitral annular calcification, which is associated with higher rates of complications and worse outcomes in valve surgery.</p>
        <p>Marked left ventricular dysfunction is also a relative contraindication, since restoring full valve competence removes the low-resistance regurgitant pathway, thereby increasing left ventricular afterload and potentially unmasking or worsening true&#x000a0;left ventricular failure; in chronic severe mitral stenosis, preoperative left ventricular ejection fraction often overestimates intrinsic contractile function because the regurgitant orifice acts as a mechanical &#x0201c;pop-off&#x0201d; valve.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r25">[25]</xref> Advanced pulmonary and respiratory disease further complicates decision-making. Severe emphysema, restrictive lung disease, and pulmonary hypertension are common in patients with long-standing mitral regurgitation and are associated with higher perioperative risk, especially when right ventricular function is impaired. In such patients, careful multidisciplinary evaluation, integrating surgical risk scores, imaging, and pulmonary assessment, is essential to balance the benefits of mitral intervention against the potential for adverse outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Mitral valve repair requires cardiopulmonary bypass and, in the vast majority of cases, full cardiac arrest. Standard equipment will include instruments necessary for open cardiac operations. Transesophageal echocardiography is mandatory for intraoperative repair and evaluation of ventricular function.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Mitral valve repair requires standard open cardiac surgical staff, which includes, at minimum, a cardiac surgeon experienced in mitral valve surgery, a cardiac anesthesiologist, a perfusionist, a surgical assistant, a surgical nurse or technician, and a circulating or operating room nurse. Postoperatively, these patients require care from a critical care intensivist, cardiologist, physical therapist, social worker, and various levels of nursing staff.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>The preoperative assessment of patients undergoing cardiac surgery requires an appraisal of the patient and procedural risks, including a thorough medical history, focused physical examination, a review of diagnostic studies, and pertinent consultant preoperative evaluations. Significant cardiovascular risk factors include myocardial ischemia, ventricular dysfunction with heart failure, and atherosclerotic disease of the carotid arteries or proximal aorta. Establishing the etiology of ventricular dysfunction is essential to optimizing perioperative hemodynamic parameters.</p>
        <p>Depending on their presentation, patients can be stratified into low-, intermediate-, or high-risk. Low-risk patients present with angina but no preoperative myocardial infarction and are scheduled for elective surgery. Intermediate-risk individuals may have an acute myocardial infarction but remain hemodynamically stable, often requiring hospitalization with heparin and antiplatelet therapy. Those at high-risk are hemodynamically unstable after an acute myocardial infarction and face a heightened risk of morbidity and mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>For those with heart failure, preoperative assessment should include evaluation of left and right ventricular dysfunction. Anesthetic planning must consider the need for intraoperative monitoring, appropriate anesthetic agents, vasoactive support, and possible mechanical assistance. Pulmonary hypertension, defined by a mean pulmonary artery pressure greater than 25 mm Hg at rest, significantly raises perioperative morbidity and mortality risks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with severe atherosclerosis of the proximal aorta or carotid arteries are at an increased risk for perioperative stroke. Additional comorbidities that must be evaluated include diabetes, hypertension, prior cerebrovascular events, peripheral vascular disease, and smoking-related pulmonary pathology. Stroke risk can be mitigated by preoperatively administering aspirin, beta-blockers, and statins. Those with extremely high stroke risk may require consultation with a vascular surgeon before surgery. During cardiac procedures, aortic plaques can dislodge due to clamping, unclamping, or turbulent blood flow, increasing the risk of embolic events such as stroke.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Noncardiac risk factors that cannot be modified include female sex, advanced age, New York Heart Association functional status, preexisting renal insufficiency, prior transient ischemic attack, anemia, and a history of tobacco use. Preexisting renal insufficiency can lead to acute kidney injury in 1% to 2% of patients, which significantly increases mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;These patients should avoid exposure to nephrotoxic agents and have their volume status optimized.</p>
        <p>Reduced cardiac output or hypotension requires prompt treatment. Anemia is a common perioperative finding in cardiac surgical patients and is independently associated with increased transfusion risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;In patients with diabetes, hypoglycemia and marked hyperglycemia should be strictly avoided to prevent perioperative complications. Patients with hypertension should have chronically administered oral antihypertensive agents until surgery, excluding angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin II receptor blockers.</p>
        <p>Patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease should be at their optimal baseline level of pulmonary function before the intervention.&#x000a0;Patients who smoke or use tobacco&#x000a0;receive counsel about preoperative cessation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r35">[35]</xref> Ensuring optimal preoperative nutrition and postoperative rehabilitation programs has improved functional capacity in older patients. For elective surgical procedures, prolonged aortic cross-clamp time and total cardiopulmonary bypass time are associated with a higher risk.</p>
        <p>The mortality risk also directly correlates with procedural volume or with the surgeon's or institution's experience.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Medical management of mitral valve pathology includes diuretics and rate-controlling agents, such as beta-blockers, for symptomatic relief. Patients with&#x000a0;atrial fibrillation should receive anticoagulation with warfarin. Vasodilators are frequently used to reduce arterial resistance and improve cardiac output, thereby increasing the mitral valve closing force and reducing backflow. Although vasodilators are effective in reducing acute mitral regurgitation, sustained improvement can be challenging.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Mitral valve repair&#x000a0;aims to restore proper valve function and improve hemodynamics in patients with mitral stenosis&#x000a0;or mitral regurgitation. Advances in surgical and percutaneous approaches have refined repair strategies, prioritizing valve preservation whenever possible. This section outlines the key procedural steps, decision-making criteria, and therapeutic interventions that guide mitral valve repair for optimal patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Standard Mitral Valve Repair</bold>
</p>
        <p>Standard mitral valve repair is performed using full cardiopulmonary bypass and ischemic arrest. Numerous possible surgical approaches exist, including median sternotomy, right thoracotomy, and robot-assisted. Regardless of the incisional approach, the core principles of mitral valve repair remain the same: creating a competent valve with good coaptation depth, ring annuloplasty, and avoiding systolic anterior motion. The surgical technique for mitral valve repair via median sternotomy is described here; this approach is essential for patients needing additional concomitant procedures such as CABG, ascending aortic intervention, or extra/multiple valve intervention:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cannulation for&#x000a0;cardiopulmonary bypass is achieved via the ascending aorta and bicaval venous cannulation. Cardioplegic arrest is achieved via antegrade and retrograde routes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>After aortic cross-clamping and adequate diastolic cardioplegic arrest, the interatrial groove (S&#x000f8;ndergaard or Waterston) is exposed. Left atriotomy is created away from the pulmonary veins. Alternatively, a transseptal approach can be performed via right atriotomy and incision of the septum at the fossa ovalis. Appropriate retractors are placed for exposure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The valve is inspected systematically using saline injection and visual inspection of each segment. The repair technique will depend on the findings of valve pathology. Isolated P2 prolapse, for instance, can be repaired by triangular or quadrangular resection and ring annuloplasty or by creating neochordae to the appropriate papillary muscle. Neochord repair has been shown to have lower rates of reoperation for recurrent mitral regurgitation than resection techniques. We recommend ring annuloplasty for all repairs.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Annuloplasty sutures are placed around the annulus, and the anterior leaflet height is sized. The ring can be true-sized or undersized depending on mitral regurgitation pathology (primary or secondary, ischemic or nonischemic). Testing the valve for coaptation depth is essential; the ideal depth&#x000a0;approximates 1 cm. Importantly, excessive undersizing of the ring should be avoided as this may cause systolic anterior motion.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Carpentier and Lawrie Techniques</bold>
</p>
        <p>Alain Carpentier&#x02019;s technique for correction of mitral regurgitation,&#x000a0;developed through autopsy and pathology studies of the mitral valve, includes leaflet repair with quadrangular resection and rigid annuloplasty to correct annular dilatation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Lawrie et al described a functional correction of mitral regurgitation that spares the valve leaflets and chordae during repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;A flexible annuloplasty ring corrects annular dilatation, and proprietary artificial fabric chordae are used to repair prolapse and realign leaflets.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;In patients with degenerative mitral regurgitation, the mitral valve annulus can enlarge, leading to flattening of the valvular orifice and reduced leaflet edge apposition.</p>
        <p>With inadequate leaflet coaptation, ventricular systole stresses the leaflet bodies and marginal chordae, leading to further dysfunction and failure. According to reports, when employing the Lawrie technique, the rates of avoidance of reoperation and of significant recurrent mitral regurgitation, as assessed by echocardiography at&#x000a0;1 year, are 90.1% and 93.9%, respectively. Also, if the ventricular filling is optimized through adequate preload and afterload, atrioventricular dyssynchrony is avoided, hypercontractility is limited, and there is almost no postoperative systolic anterior movement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Braunberger et al reported long-term results of valve repair in nonrheumatic mitral regurgitation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r38">[38]</xref> In patients with isolated posterior leaflet prolapse, 10- and 20-year freedom from reoperation was 98.5% and 96.9%, respectively. In patients with isolated anterior prolapse, the figures were 86.2% and 82.2%, respectively. In patients with bileaflet prolapse, freedom from reoperation was 88.1% and 82.6%, respectively. These data confirm the stability of the Carpentier repair over an extended period.</p>
        <p>
<bold>PMBC</bold>
</p>
        <p>Randomized trials have demonstrated that PMBC is safe and effective compared to surgical closed or open commissurotomy in patients with favorable valve morphology characterized by less than 2+ mitral regurgitation and no left atrial thrombus. During PMBC,&#x000a0;1 or more balloon catheters are advanced across the mitral valve and inflated to split the commissures. Ideal candidates for PMBC have mobile, relatively thin, calcium-free valve leaflets without significant subvalvular fusion. An anatomic mitral morphology score can help determine a patient&#x02019;s suitability for PMBC by assessing the appearance of the commissures and the extent of calcification. Clinical factors such as age, New York Heart Association class, and presence or absence of atrial fibrillation also predict outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Commissurotomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mitral valve surgery is a well-established treatment for rheumatic mitral stenosis, with commissurotomy being the preferred method when the valvular anatomy is suitable. This procedure can be performed as a closed commissurotomy, in which the valve is opened without direct visualization of the left atrium or left ventricular, or as an open commissurotomy, which allows for more extensive surgery under direct observation. However, mitral valve replacement may be the most appropriate option for significant valvular thickening and subvalvular fibrosis with associated leaflet tethering. For patients with suboptimal valve anatomy, a history of failed PMBC, or moderate-to-severe tricuspid regurgitation, a surgical approach that includes tricuspid valve repair may yield better outcomes. Additional procedures, such as atrial fibrillation ablation and tricuspid valve repair, are strongly recommended (class 1) in conjunction with mitral valve replacement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Minimally Invasive Mitral Valve Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Minimally invasive mitral valve surgery (MIMVS) can be divided into&#x000a0;2 groups: partial sternotomy and right thoracotomy, including the open and video-assisted methods, with or without robotic assistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;This procedure has been shown to reduce surgical trauma by avoiding a full sternotomy incision. MIMVS approaches will require different cannulation techniques, generally involving the femoral vessels. S&#x000fc;ndermann et al showed equivalent short- and mid-term outcomes with MIMVS, as compared to conventional surgery, regarding the incidence of stroke, mortality, and durability of repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients who undergo MIMVS have reduced blood loss, a reduced need for blood transfusion, shorter time on mechanical ventilation, shorter intensive care unit stay, and a quicker resumption of regular activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;Iribarne et al reported that MIMVS is associated with lower hospital costs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r42">[42]</xref> Due to these highly optimistic results, the proportion of MIMVS increased from 10% in 2004 to 20% in 2008. The least invasive approach for MIMVS is robotically-assisted, eliminating the need for thoracotomy or significant rib spreading.&#x000a0;However, this method has higher operative costs. The robotically-assisted approach allows for excellent 3-dimensional visualization of the valvular and subvalvular apparatus, thanks to EndoWrist&#x000a0;technology, which enables complex surgical maneuvers.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Transapical Beating-Heart Mitral Valve Repair</bold>
</p>
        <p>An innovative approach for treating degenerative mitral valve disease due to posterior leaflet prolapse is transapical beating-heart mitral valve repair with polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) chordae implantation. This procedure avoids cardiopulmonary bypass by accessing the cardiac apex through a small left anterolateral thoracotomy incision at the fourth or fifth intercostal space. The neochordae device lets the surgeon grasp and pierce the prolapsing leaflet, pulling the PTFE chord through the segment.</p>
        <p>The neochordae are then externalized at the cardiac apex and adjusted under echocardiographic guidance to ensure maximal coaptation and resolution of mitral regurgitation. Early follow-up has shown that most patients are stable. This technique is used to treat mitral regurgitation due to prolapsing lesions early in the disease course, when annular dilatation is minimal, and left ventricular remodeling is limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r42">[42]</xref> Transcatheter techniques targeting both the leaflets and annulus are increasingly employed to achieve durable results in appropriate patients. However, omitting ring annuloplasty is associated with poor long-term outcomes, underscoring the importance of careful patient selection.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Recurrent mitral regurgitation is the most common complication following primary mitral valve repair. The pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) and left atrium pressures are equivalent. The a&#x000a0;wave and v&#x000a0;wave are key components of heart pressure, and variations in their size can provide critical diagnostic insights.</p>
        <p>The significance of the v&#x000a0;wave is linked to any pathological condition that impacts atrial filling during systole when the atrioventricular valves are closed. The size of the v&#x000a0;wave is partially influenced by the volume of blood entering the atrium, with&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation being the most common cause of large v&#x000a0;waves. In cases of acute mitral regurgitation, such as with a ruptured chordae tendineae, giant v&#x000a0;waves can occur.</p>
        <p>V&#x000a0;waves more than twice the mean PCWP indicate severe mitral regurgitation. As an example, in severe mitral paravalvular leak, elevated mean&#x000a0;left atrium pressure and prominent v&#x000a0;waves can lead to pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure. Closing the paravalvular leak typically results in a rapid reduction in v waves and mean left atrial pressure, improvement in the echocardiographic grade of regurgitation, and immediate positive effects on the lungs and right heart.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Intraoperative assessment of the repaired valve with TEE is crucial because it enables immediate evaluation. If persistent mild-to-greater mitral regurgitation is present, the valve must be reexplored for re-repair or replacement. This decision is critical in cases of impaired left ventricular function, as these patients may not tolerate a long repeat ischemic/arrest period.</p>
        <p>The long-term durability of a mitral valve repair is more difficult to predict. Flameng et al reported that only 50% of patients remained free of mitral valve incompetence&#x000a0;7 years following repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a study by Chikwe et al&#x000a0;demonstrated that mitral valve repair did not confer a long-term survival benefit in patients over 60 who required concomitant CABG surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;Fifty-nine octogenarians undergoing mitral valve surgery for nonrheumatic disease demonstrated similar outcomes between repair and replacement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r46">[46]</xref> A national Society of Thoracic Surgeons database analysis included 8523 mitral valve repairs and 3520 replacements and concluded that operative mortality was lower in the repair cohort than in the replacement cohort, with and without chordal preservation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>Following successful mitral valve repair, patients with&#x000a0;pulmonary hypertension and atrial fibrillation&#x000a0;have worse long-term survival and event-free survival, as well as increased compromise of the repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;Based on findings by the Mayo Clinic group, postoperative atrial fibrillation occurs in up to 24% of patients previously in sinus rhythm, particularly those with&#x000a0;left atrium enlargement, and is associated with increased mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;There has been a recent trend to perform surgical ablation of atrial fibrillation during mitral valve repair to aid with this complication.</p>
        <p>Up to 32.2% of patients presenting for repair have atrial fibrillation, with concomitant atrial fibrillation ablation occurring in 61.5% of patients, according to the Society of Thoracic Surgeons database.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Gillinov et al reported that adding atrial fibrillation ablation to mitral valve surgery increased freedom from atrial fibrillation at 1 year (63.2% vs 39.4%), with similar mortality in both groups. Still, pacemaker implantation increased in the ablation group.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve can result if there is a discrepancy between the annular area and leaflet tissue following repair. Systolic anterior motion results from anterior mitral valve prolapse into the LVOT during systole&#x000a0;and most commonly occurs with an undersized annuloplasty ring or excess leaflet tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;Systolic anterior motion can lead to residual&#x000a0;mitral regurgitation and LVOT obstruction, both of which are observable on intraoperative TEE.</p>
        <p>If systolic anterior motion is seen intraoperatively following the repair, ventricular filling should be optimized immediately, atrioventricular pacing should be implemented to improve atrioventricular synchrony, and ventricular hypercontractility should be reduced. Postoperative beta blockers are helpful in this regard. David and colleagues report that freedom from mitral regurgitation ranges from 65% to 80% at 12 years postoperatively, depending on which mitral valve leaflet suffered prolapse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Jouan et al describe a 9.8% recurrence rate of moderate or severe mitral regurgitation in patients with Barlow disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r54">[54]</xref></p>
        <p>Iatrogenic injury to the circumflex artery following mitral valve surgery can occur due to the artery's proximity to the anterolateral commissure of the mitral annulus, particularly in the mid-to-distal segment. This risk is heightened in cases of Barlow disease, where annular dilatation and hypermobility of the valvular leaflets make it challenging to predict the precise location of the circumflex artery, thereby increasing the risk of coronary artery injury. The limited data on the incidence of this complication, which has been reported to range from 0.3% to 1.8%, complicates the development of effective strategies for its early detection and management. The likelihood of iatrogenic myocardial infarction due to&#x000a0;arterial injury is slightly higher following mitral valve repair (2.2%) compared to isolated mitral valve replacement (1.7%), with no significant decrease in incidence during minimally invasive mitral valve repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>While the urgent restoration of coronary blood flow is crucial, no universally established treatment protocol exists. The choice between PCI and surgical myocardial revascularization typically depends on the specific situation. If the left circumflex artery injury is identified during surgery, immediate surgical myocardial revascularization is generally advised. However, PCI is often preferred when the injury is detected postoperatively because it is quicker and avoids the need for a repeat sternotomy. Nonetheless, surgical intervention should be considered if technical challenges arise during PCI, such as difficulty navigating the lesion, incomplete balloon expansion, or failure to restore blood flow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>Atrioventricular&#x000a0;groove disruption is a severe complication that can occur due to surgical techniques or stretch injury resulting from removing the posterior leaflet and the subsequent untethering of the left ventricle. This complication occurs in approximately 1.2% of mitral valve replacements and carries a mortality rate of up to 75%. Repairing this disruption is challenging, with both external and internal repair approaches documented.</p>
        <p>Although internal approaches are generally preferred, achieving reliable exposure for the repair can be difficult.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r57">[57]</xref> Age is a recognized risk factor for&#x000a0;atrioventricular groove rupture due to the higher prevalence of annular calcification and fragile tissues in older adults. Preserving the posterior leaflet and basal chordae can reduce the risk of&#x000a0;atrioventricular groove rupture, though this is not always feasible. While certain types of&#x000a0;atrioventricular&#x000a0;dissociation might be managed through atriotomy, complete perforation and rupture present significant risks if not adequately exposed. This challenge arises in part from the complexity of the surgical approach and the proximity of the circumflex artery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r58">[58]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>With advanced age, degenerative mitral valve disease is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation. Mitral valve repair is the gold standard for degenerative mitral regurgitation and is recommended in current guidelines for the management of&#x000a0;valvular heart disease. American and European guidelines use the same criteria to define left ventricular dysfunction in patients with primary mitral regurgitation, with thresholds of LVEF at less than 60% or left ventricular end-systolic diameter less than 40 mm.</p>
        <p>There is strong consensus between the guidelines regarding mitral valve surgery for symptomatic individuals with severe primary mitral regurgitation, with both recommending it as a class I intervention, regardless of LV function. Surgery is also advised in both sets of guidelines (class I) for asymptomatic patients who exhibit LV dysfunction as defined by these criteria. Additionally, both guidelines prefer mitral valve repair over replacement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>The ACC/AHA guidelines recommend that surgery take place before left ventricular dysfunction (class IIa) in experienced centers. Mitral regurgitation imposes significant volume overload on the left ventricle, leading to permanent structural and functional deterioration of the myocardium and subsequent heart failure. Timely correction of mitral regurgitation is paramount for preserving cardiac function. Minimally invasive mitral valve surgery and transcatheter mitral valve repair technologies have been instrumental in allowing the repair of pathology in high-risk individuals who are not appropriate for open-heart surgery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Mitral regurgitation is the second most common valvular heart disease after aortic stenosis in the general population and requires an interprofessional healthcare team for management. In industrialized countries, the most common etiology is degenerative mitral valve disease, leading to prolapse caused by either myxomatous degeneration or fibroelastic deficiency. Mitral valve repair is preferred over&#x000a0;replacement if complete and durable repair can be achieved.</p>
        <p>While the natural history of this valvular pathology is poor, leading to eventual left ventricular decompensation, an appropriate and timely repair can be lifesaving and prolong life expectancy to that of the healthy age-matched population. Mortality is increased in patients with symptoms of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Transcatheter techniques may be challenging in patients with certain anatomical limitations, including calcified leaflets and advanced disease. Repair is not always practical, and patient selection is imperative to prevent the recurrence of mitral regurgitation.</p>
        <p>The success of the repair depends partially on the center and the surgeon&#x02019;s level of experience. Intraoperative collaboration with cardiac anesthesia and the use of TEE is critical. Current guidelines recommend that these procedures be performed at &#x0201c;Heart Valve Centers of Excellence&#x0201d; (HVCEs), which offer comprehensive options for diagnosing and managing valvular disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r60">[60]</xref>&#x000a0;An HVCE should deliver higher-quality care due to a higher volume of repair procedures, advanced imaging techniques, and greater transparency regarding patient outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;An annual surgical volume of fewer than 25 cases is associated with a lower repair rate, increased 1-year mortality, and a higher incidence of subsequent reoperation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-623.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>An interprofessional team dedicated to these patients should include cardiologists, anesthesiologists, nurse anesthetists, and intensivists. Cardiology specialty nursing should be available to assist at every step, both perioperatively and during procedures, collaborating with clinicians and specialists to provide monitoring and patient and family education. Perioperative evaluation should include high-quality TEE with 3-dimensional imaging.</p>
        <p>Goals for repair should include mortality below 1% for isolated repair, a near 100% repair rate, and less than 5% repair failure at 5-year follow-up. Centers should be involved in research and innovation of techniques for procedural improvement. A successful HVCE should adhere to international guidelines, engage in the appropriate and timely referral of patients, evaluate and enhance patient outcomes, and participate in regional or national outcome registries.&#x000a0;With an interprofessional team approach, including specialists, clinicians, nurses, and pharmacists, patient outcomes can improve, and adverse events can be minimized, resulting in better quality of life and outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-623.s13" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>The nurse&#x000a0;is crucial to the care of the postoperative patient.&#x000a0;Their duties include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Educating the patient on the course of the illness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Encouraging incentive spirometry</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Helping the patient to ambulate</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Offering and encouraging a healthy diet</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ensuring medication compliance&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring vital signs and neurological exam</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring&#x000a0;fluid balance</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Checking daily labs and&#x000a0;radiographs</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring output from the mediastinal tubes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Wound care</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
